WP19.2 -- NodalArena::adoptCanonical under _mutex, plus an adopt-race counter.

WHY THIS IS A PATCH FILE AND NOT A COMMIT.  src/CudaXsReconBackend.cu is held by
the concurrent WP20 FP32 arm; this diff is written against the file as it stands
at 0054838 (`git show 0054838:src/CudaXsReconBackend.cu`) and is to be applied
after WP20 lands:

    git apply --3way docs/patches/wp19_2_adopt_canonical.patch

WHAT IT CHANGES, IN THREE PLACES.

 1. `g_nodal_adopt_races` -- a new process-wide counter beside the other nodal
    batch counters.
 2. `NodalArena::refreshViews()` -- takes `_mutex` for the whole read of
    `_canon[]` and the clear of `_views_dirty`.  Its one caller is
    launchBatch(), which drive() reaches only after `lock.unlock()`, so this is
    a fresh acquisition on a non-recursive mutex and NOT a self-deadlock.
 3. `NodalArena::adoptCanonical()` -- takes the same `_mutex` around the
    `_canon[slot]` write and the `_views_dirty` set, and bumps the counter when
    it enters the window that used to lose an adoption.

WHAT IT COSTS.  One uncontended mutex per adoption and per view refresh.  Both
are stand-up-frequency events (an adoption happens when a slot's canonical set
changes; a refresh happens only when the table is dirty), not per-outer, so this
is not in the solve path.

VERIFICATION.  `[RASBERY][NODAL_ARENA] {"adopt_races_detected":N,"canon_locked":1}`
is printed once per process at teardown, unconditionally, so `0` is a statement.
A soak that reports a nonzero N on THIS tree measured the window and lost
nothing; the same N on a tree without the lock is N chances to have lost an
adoption.

